 "notes": "Libretto by Luigi Illica and Giuseppe Giacosa (1896). Words follow the Ricordi vocal score's spelling and punctuation (terribil, cagion, tepor, Chè?!, m'ássale with the print's accent, M'hai sung on two notes as M'ha-i; the print's runs of dots after scuote, gote, di, sangue... rosse, squallida, spento, vento, Ahimè, finita, vita). \"Imparerai.\" is one word over the print's \"Im-pa-re.\" (bar 57, with a stray full stop) + \"ra-i.\" (bar 58). Marcello's \"Ah la civetta! Imparerai.\" is marked parlato but printed with pitches; it is given as sung words like any other. IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, words sung on one note joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ, intervocalic s voiced; io on one note is jo; paura is sung on three notes (pa-u-ra), Imparerai on five (im-pa-re-ra-i). Translation literal, for meaning.",
